WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.900
Network parsers in C deserve careful memory-safety design because every byte comes from outside the process boundary.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:17.240
The parser should treat input as untrusted data until it has validated length, type, format, encoding, bounds, conversion results, and protocol state.

3
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:22.460
Validation is not a single check; it is a sequence of checks before each meaningful use.

4
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.780
Buffers should always travel with accurate length values.

5
00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:30.200
A pointer without a length invites hidden assumptions.

6
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.620
A length without an ownership model invites leaks or double cleanup.

7
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:45.120
Parser code should make it clear which buffer owns memory, which view is borrowed, how much capacity is available, how many bytes are currently valid, and when the memory is released.

8
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:49.720
Integer conversions are a common source of parser mistakes.

9
00:00:49.820 --> 00:01:01.500
Lengths may arrive as text, binary fields, signed values, unsigned values, network-byte-order values, or values that need multiplication or addition before allocation.

10
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:12.320
The code should check conversion success, range, overflow, and maximum policy before using a value for allocation, indexing, copying, or pointer movement.

11
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.920
String handling needs explicit boundaries.

12
00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:22.540
Network data is not automatically null-terminated, and embedded zero bytes may be meaningful or invalid depending on the protocol.

13
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:30.640
Code should not pass untrusted byte ranges to string functions unless it has created a safe representation with known length and termination behavior.

14
00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:33.960
Malformed data should be rejected before deeper processing.

15
00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:46.780
The parser should avoid partially applying a command, allocating large state, writing logs with uncontrolled content, or advancing the connection state until the frame is known to be meaningful for the current protocol state.

16
00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:52.240
Rejection should release temporary resources and leave the connection in a defined condition.

17
00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.840
Keep parser state small and reviewable.

18
00:01:54.940 --> 00:02:02.860
A compact state machine with clear ownership is easier to test than a parser spread across unrelated callbacks and globals.

19
00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:12.160
Tests should include valid frames, incomplete frames, boundary sizes, malformed fields, conversion failures, and cleanup after rejection.
